---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Critical values — exuber"
  description="There's no closed-form distribution for GSADF-family statistics — how thresholds are simulated instead, and where that's headed."
>
  <Nav active="critical-values" />
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6">
    <section class="py-16 sm:py-20">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-explosive">Critical values</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 font-display text-4xl leading-[1.15] text-ink">Thresholds simulated, not looked up.</h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-muted">
        ADF has a tabulated asymptotic distribution because Dickey &amp; Fuller solved it
        analytically. GSADF's sup-of-a-sequence has no such closed form — the only way to a
        threshold is to generate data under the null and see where the statistic actually lands.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-border py-16 sm:py-20">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">Monte Carlo</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-2xl text-ink">Simulate the null directly.</h2>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Draw a pure random walk — <code class="text-ink">cumsum(rnorm(n))</code>, nothing else — run
        GSADF on it, and repeat. A few thousand replications later, the 90th/95th/99th percentile
        of that empirical distribution is the critical value at that sample size. Exact for that T
        and <code class="text-ink">minw</code>, but assumes homoskedastic Gaussian innovations,
        which real series rarely have.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        <code class="text-ink">exuber</code>'s bundled table was built exactly this way: 2,000
        replications, sample sizes 6 through 600, seeded for reproducibility
        (<code class="text-ink">data-raw/sim-crit.R</code>) — then shipped as a lookup table so a
        typical <code class="text-ink">radf()</code> call doesn't re-simulate anything.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-border py-16 sm:py-20">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">Wild bootstrap</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-2xl text-ink">Simulate the null from your data.</h2>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Instead of drawing fresh Gaussian noise, fit the null model to the actual series, take its
        residuals, and resample them with random ± sign weights each replication. Whatever
        heteroskedasticity is really in the data — volatility clustering, a variance break, fat
        tails — survives into the simulated critical value instead of being assumed away.
        Correspondingly more expensive: it's specific to one dataset, not reusable across series.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-border py-16 sm:py-20">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">Where this is headed</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-2xl text-ink">One shared table, not three.</h2>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Right now <code class="text-ink">exuber</code> bundles its own precomputed <code class="text-ink">.rda</code>,
        and <code class="text-ink">pyexuber</code> has none yet — each binding would otherwise end up
        re-simulating and re-bundling the same numbers separately. The plan is a standalone,
        versioned critical-value table, computed once and served rather than duplicated, so
        <code class="text-ink">pyexuber</code> and this site can read the same thresholds
        <code class="text-ink">exuber</code> does.
      </p>
      <span class="mt-6 inline-block rounded-full border border-dashed border-border px-3 py-1 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">
        shared critical-value service — planned
      </span>
    </section>
  </div>
  <Footer />
</Base>
